MSVC treats C4244 (narrowing conversion) as an error, causing the `msvc-debug` CI job to fail when building `pixel_traits.t.cpp`. The issue is a range-for loop initializing `uint16_t h` from a brace-init list of `unsigned int` literals (`0x0001U`, etc.), which MSVC flags as a narrowing conversion.

## Changes

- **`src/media/t/pixel_traits.t.cpp`**: Explicitly type the initializer list as `std::initializer_list<std::uint16_t>` to eliminate the implicit narrowing conversion:

```cpp
// Before — deduced as initializer_list<unsigned int>, narrowed into uint16_t h
for (const std::uint16_t h : {0x0001U, 0x0002U, 0x0100U, 0x0200U, 0x03FFU, 0x8001U, 0x83FFU}) {

// After — no narrowing, types match exactly
for (const std::uint16_t h :
     std::initializer_list<std::uint16_t>{0x0001U, 0x0002U, 0x0100U, 0x0200U, 0x03FFU, 0x8001U, 0x83FFU}) {
```

- Adds `#include <initializer_list>` since the type is now used explicitly.

Several tests slept a fixed duration and then took a single snapshot of an asynchronous result, racing whatever they were waiting on:

- `plugin/forward` health tests (`TestHealth`, `TestHealthTCP`, `TestHealthNoRecursion`) slept 20ms for the health-check goroutine to bump an atomic counter to 1;
- `test/auto` `TestAuto` slept 50ms for the `auto` plugin file-watch reload to pick up an added/removed zone;
- `test/metrics` `TestMetricsAuto` slept 110ms before scraping Prometheus metrics after a reload;
- `plugin/file` `TestZoneReload` slept 30ms — the comment even said *"Could still be racy"* — for a reload to be observed;
- `plugin/health` `Test_health_overloaded_cancellation` slept 1s for its background goroutine to fire its first request.

Each is replaced with a bounded poll of the actual condition (the atomic counter, a re-issued `dns.Exchange`, a metrics scrape, `z.ApexIfDefined`, or a channel signalled by the test's own HTTP handler), so the test proceeds as soon as the awaited state is reached and no longer flakes when it happens to be slower than the fixed wait under load. All existing assertions are preserved.

I deliberately left absence-style checks untouched — e.g. `TestHealthNoMaxFails` asserts the counter stays `0`, where polling would be wrong.

Test-only; no plugin behaviour changed. `gofmt`/`go vet` clean; the affected tests pass under `-count`.

## Audit findings

The account quest API normalized quest identifiers but did not require them to exist in `account_quests.lua`. Callers could therefore create access or reward rows for arbitrary syntactically valid IDs. The startup log also used Lua's length operator on a string-keyed table and reported zero registered quests.

The access integration had a more serious progression issue: using any mapped early quest door unlocked the single account-wide flag for the whole quest. That could let alternate characters pass later doors before the original character had completed the quest.

## Fix

- fail closed when `quests` is not a table;
- require a registered quest definition in access and reward APIs;
- count string-keyed quest definitions with `pairs`;
- stop ordinary quest doors from unlocking whole-quest account access;
- unlock Secret Service account access only at the common Mission 07 completion gate;
- keep The Ape City unlock tied to its existing final catacomb access check;
- add a contract validator that checks configured IDs, integrations, permission boundaries and completion-only unlock rules;
- add validator regression tests and a focused workflow.

## Scope

This does not make every quest storage account-wide. It hardens the existing opt-in completion/access layer for quests explicitly registered in `data-otservbr-global/account_quests.lua`.

